@@ -446,6 +446,10 @@ The library also supports convenient formatting of ranges and tuples::
   // Prints "('a', 1, 2.0)"
   fmt::print("{}", t);
 
+Character ranges are formatted as sequences of quoted characters, including
+when nested in other ranges. This also applies to C++20 range views such as
+``std::views::split``. A range is not treated as a string merely because it can
+be explicitly converted to a string view.
 
 NOTE: currently, the overload of ``fmt::join`` for iterables exists in the main
 ``format.h`` header, but expect this to change in the future.
